  "narrative": {
    "intro": "North Carolina has $4.2B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. Obligations are down 55.3% year over year. The state has 153,408 awards so far and an average award size of $27.2K. Fiscal-year comparisons show a peak in FY25 at $9.3B and a decline to $4.2B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $1.8B, followed by the General Services Administration with $569M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $545M. Research Triangle Institute leads vendors at $199M, followed by SDV Office Systems LLC at $177M and General Dynamics Information Technology, Inc. at $176M. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry by obligations at $387M, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $309M and Engineering Services at $270M. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in North Carolina trended over recent years?",
        "body": "Obligations rose from $6.7B in FY21 to $9.3B in FY25, then fell to $4.2B in FY26. Obligations are down 55.3% year over year, reflecting the partial nature of FY26 totals. The state has 153,408 awards this fiscal year with an average award size of $27.2K. Historical fiscal-year totals provide context for the FY26 decline."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors account for the most federal spending in North Carolina?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer at $1.8B, followed by the General Services Administration at $569M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $545M. Research Triangle Institute is the top vendor at $199M, followed by SDV Office Systems LLC at $177M and General Dynamics Information Technology, Inc. at $176M. Computer Systems Design Services leads industries at $387M, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $309M and Engineering Services at $270M."
      }
    ]
  },
